# Zephyr's default log ring is 1024 B. That is not enough for the ESP32-C3's boot
# burst: MCUboot's own load lines and the ESP HAL chatter fill it before the log
# thread drains anything, and the first application messages of every boot - the
# identity block and the build banner - were being dropped (three captures in a row
# showed nothing of it, while the same build with 2048 B showed all three lines).
# The ESP32-C5 cannot afford the extra kilobyte: its SRAM links with about 3,200
# bytes free (measured), so it keeps the default and its boot log stays lossy.
config LOG_BUFFER_SIZE
	default 2048 if !BOARD_HCP_C5

config HCP_BENCH
	bool "Bench / HIL firmware: bring-up aids on, verbose logging"
	default n
	help
	  A build meant to be run on the test bench rather than shipped: device-domain
	  logging is debug so the boot is readable without a serial session, and the
	  bring-up aids in src/app_demos.cpp are available. hcp::provision, MQTT, SSDP,
	  OTA and the collector stay in, so a bench device is reachable and upgradeable
	  over the network - which is the point: after the first flash it should never
	  need the USB port again.

	  ./build.sh <board> --bench

	  The aids are still each behind their own switch and none is turned on by this
	  symbol. Two of them want pins the product path is already using: the Modbus
	  poll demo would be a second master on the bus hcp::collector owns, and the raw
	  loopback test drives the same uart1 TX/RX. The LED colour cycle needs the strip
	  to itself, so it only compiles when hcp::provision is off (a status light and a
	  demo cannot share one pin) - add -DCONFIG_HCP_PROVISION=n for that.

	  The product build compiles none of this in, and the boot banner says which
	  image is running.

config APP_VERSION
	string "firmware version (dotted numeric: major.minor.patch.build)"
	default "2.0.3.20"
	help
	  The one version knob of a build. Reported on the <sn>/online topic and used
	  by hcp::ota as "what am I running", so an update is only taken when the
	  server offers something above it (hcp::ota_client parses up to four dotted
	  fields, so 2.0.3.14 compares correctly against an earlier 2.0.3.13).

	  MCUboot compares the version in the image header
	  (CONFIG_MCUBOOT_IMGTOOL_SIGN_VERSION), not this string, so the two have to
	  move together: ./build.sh <board> --version <x.y.z.w> sets both. It spells
	  the header version as x.y.z+w because that is all imgtool's regex accepts -
	  see the note in prj.conf.

	  The release line started at 2.0.0.0; it is deliberately four fields so a
	  build number can be bumped without touching the version the hardware contract
	  (Home Assistant's firmware sensor) sees as major.minor.patch.

source "Kconfig.zephyr"
